---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How Mouse DPI Measurement Works | Mouse DPI Analyzer"
  description="Learn how mouse DPI is measured, what DPI means, why it matters for gaming and productivity, and how our Pointer Lock API approach ensures accurate results."
>
  <Navbar />

  <main class="content-page">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="content-layout">
        <!-- Main Content -->
        <article class="content-main">
          <div class="page-header">
            <span class="caption-mono" style="color: var(--color-mute);">Guide</span>
            <h1 class="display-lg" style="margin: 8px 0 0;">How DPI measurement works.</h1>
            <p class="body-lg" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 12px 0 0;">
              Understanding mouse DPI, how we measure it, and why our approach delivers accurate results.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- What is DPI -->
          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="display-md">What is mouse DPI?</h2>
            <p class="body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              DPI stands for <strong>Dots Per Inch</strong>. It measures how many pixels your cursor moves on screen for every inch your mouse physically travels on your desk. A mouse set to 800 DPI moves the cursor 800 pixels for every inch of physical movement.
            </p>
            <div class="info-callout card-soft">
              <div class="callout-header">
                <span class="caption-mono" style="color: var(--color-link);">Example</span>
              </div>
              <p class="body-sm" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 8px 0 0;">
                At <strong>800 DPI</strong>, moving your mouse 2 inches to the right moves the cursor <strong>1,600 pixels</strong> to the right. At <strong>1600 DPI</strong>, the same 2-inch movement moves the cursor <strong>3,200 pixels</strong>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- DPI vs Sensitivity -->
          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="display-md">DPI vs sensitivity.</h2>
            <p class="body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              DPI is a hardware setting — it's determined by your mouse's sensor. Sensitivity is a software multiplier applied by your operating system or game. They work together:
            </p>
            <div class="comparison-table card">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th class="caption-mono">Aspect</th>
                    <th class="caption-mono">DPI</th>
                    <th class="caption-mono">Sensitivity</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="body-sm" style="font-weight: 500;">Type</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Hardware (sensor)</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Software (OS/game)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="body-sm" style="font-weight: 500;">Resolution</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">More data points</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Multiplies existing data</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="body-sm" style="font-weight: 500;">Precision</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Higher = more precise</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Higher = less precise</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="body-sm" style="font-weight: 500;">Quality</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Native sensor data</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Can cause pixel skipping</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              For the best accuracy, use a higher DPI with lower in-game sensitivity rather than a low DPI with high sensitivity. The sensor captures more movement data at higher DPI.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- How We Measure -->
          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="display-md">How we measure DPI.</h2>
            <p class="body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              Our tool captures raw mouse movement data directly from your hardware using modern browser technology — bypassing any OS-level processing that could distort the result. Here's the process from your perspective:
            </p>

            <div class="steps-list">
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-marker">
                  <span class="code-text">1</span>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4 class="body-md" style="font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 4px;">Calibrate your screen</h4>
                  <p class="body-sm" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 0;">
                    Enter your screen's diagonal size so the tool knows how many physical inches correspond to your screen's pixels. This is what makes the measurement real-world accurate.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-marker">
                  <span class="code-text">2</span>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4 class="body-md" style="font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 4px;">Set a target distance</h4>
                  <p class="body-sm" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 0;">
                    Choose how far you'll physically move your mouse — 5 inches is a good starting point. Use the on-screen ruler as your reference guide.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-marker">
                  <span class="code-text">3</span>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4 class="body-md" style="font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 4px;">Move and measure</h4>
                  <p class="body-sm" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 0;">
                    Click the test area to begin, move your mouse the target distance, then click again to finish. The tool processes the raw movement data and calculates your DPI instantly.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Why Pointer Lock -->
          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="display-md">Why Pointer Lock API?</h2>
            <p class="body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              Most online DPI testers track cursor position on screen, which has several problems:
            </p>
            <ul class="body-md content-list" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              <li><strong>Screen edge clipping:</strong> The cursor stops at screen boundaries, losing movement data</li>
              <li><strong>OS acceleration:</strong> Most operating systems apply mouse acceleration by default, distorting results</li>
              <li><strong>Sub-pixel rounding:</strong> Cursor positions are integers, losing fractional pixel precision</li>
              <li><strong>Window constraints:</strong> The test area's size limits how far you can move</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="body-md" style="color: var(--color-body);">
              The Pointer Lock API bypasses all of these. It reports raw hardware movement deltas — the actual number of units the mouse sensor detected. The cursor is locked and invisible; only the raw data matters.
            </p>
            <div class="info-callout card-soft">
              <div class="callout-header">
                <span class="caption-mono" style="color: var(--color-warning);">Important</span>
              </div>
              <p class="body-sm" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 8px 0 0;">
                For the most accurate results, disable mouse acceleration in your OS settings. On Windows: Settings → Mouse → Additional mouse options → uncheck "Enhance pointer precision." On macOS, use a tool like <em>LinearMouse</em> or the terminal to disable acceleration.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Common DPI Values -->
          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="display-md">Common DPI settings.</h2>
            <div class="comparison-table card">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th class="caption-mono">DPI</th>
                    <th class="caption-mono">Use Case</th>
                    <th class="caption-mono">Common For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="code-text">400</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Competitive FPS gaming</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">CS2, Valorant pros</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="code-text">800</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">FPS gaming / general use</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Most popular DPI</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="code-text">1600</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Balanced gaming / work</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">High-res displays</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="code-text">3200+</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">Design / multi-monitor</td>
                    <td class="body-sm">4K+ displays</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- CTA -->
          <section class="content-section" style="text-align: center; padding: 48px 0;">
            <a href="/dpi-analyzer" class="btn btn-primary">
              Test Your DPI Now
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
                <polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
              </svg>
            </a>
          </section>
        </article>

        <!-- Sidebar -->
        <aside class="content-sidebar">
          <div class="sidebar-card card-soft">
            <h4 class="caption-mono" style="margin: 0 0 16px; color: var(--color-mute);">On this page</h4>
            <nav class="sidebar-nav">
              <a href="#" class="sidebar-link body-sm">What is mouse DPI?</a>
              <a href="#" class="sidebar-link body-sm">DPI vs sensitivity</a>
              <a href="#" class="sidebar-link body-sm">How we measure DPI</a>
              <a href="#" class="sidebar-link body-sm">Why Pointer Lock API?</a>
              <a href="#" class="sidebar-link body-sm">Common DPI settings</a>
            </nav>
          </div>

          <div class="sidebar-card card" style="margin-top: 16px;">
            <h4 class="body-sm" style="font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 8px;">Ready to test?</h4>
            <p class="body-sm" style="color: var(--color-body); margin: 0 0 16px;">
              Try our DPI analyzer tool. Free, instant, and accurate.
            </p>
            <a href="/dpi-analyzer" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" style="width: 100%;">
              Open DPI Analyzer
            </a>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .content-page {
    padding: 48px 0 0;
  }

  .page-header {
    margin-bottom: 48px;
    padding-bottom: 32px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .content-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 260px;
    gap: 48px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .content-section {
    margin-bottom: 48px;
  }

  .content-section h2 {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  .content-section p {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  .content-list {
    padding-left: 24px;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  .content-list li {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .info-callout {
    padding: 20px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }

  .comparison-table {
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }

  .comparison-table table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  .comparison-table th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    background: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .comparison-table td {
    padding: 10px 16px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .comparison-table tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .steps-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }

  .step-item {
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .step-marker {
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-on-primary);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    background: var(--color-canvas-soft-2);
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Sidebar */
  .content-sidebar {
    position: sticky;
    top: 84px;
  }

  .sidebar-card {
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .sidebar-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .sidebar-link {
    color: var(--color-body);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
    padding: 4px 0;
  }

  .sidebar-link:hover {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .content-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .content-sidebar {
      position: static;
      order: -1;
    }
  }
</style>
